course: dictionary
lesson: 9628
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the sentence around the word tells you which one is meant. Stud is a good example. «Football boots have studs that dig into grass» means the small metal or plastic bumps on the sole of a boot.
explain: Now read «Since 1958 LEGO bricks have used the same stud-and-tube fit.» Here a stud is the small bump on top of a LEGO brick, which clicks into the tube on the brick above. The clue is the word fit: the words around stud tell you it is about joining things together.
explain: Some meanings of stud are very different. «She wore a stud in each ear» means a small earring that sits on the earlobe, not one that hangs down. «The mare went to the stud» means a farm where horses are kept for breeding. When you meet stud, swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A stud is a small piece of metal or plastic sticking out of a surface, like the studs on football boots.
fact: A stud can also be a small earring that sits on the earlobe, or a farm where horses are kept for breeding.
fact: To be studded with something is to be covered with many small bits of it, like a sky studded with stars.
fact: «Football boots have studs that dig into grass», so the studs help players grip the ground.
fact: Since 1958 LEGO bricks have used the same stud-and-tube fit, so very old bricks still click onto new ones.
exercise: «Shoe soles have bumps that grip the ground, and football boots have studs that dig into grass.» What is a stud in this sentence? | *A small metal or plastic bump that sticks out | A jewel in a crown | A small earring | A farm for horses
exercise: «Since 1958 LEGO bricks have used the same stud-and-tube fit.» What does stud mean here? | A wooden beam in a wall | A pair of earrings | A farm where horses are bred | *A small bump on top of a brick that clicks into a tube
exercise: «The sky was studded with stars.» What does studded mean here? | Hidden from view | Hit hard with a stick | Made of wood | *Covered with many small bits, like stars
test: «Lily wore a stud in each ear, so her earrings did not dangle.» What is a stud here? | A farm where horses are kept | *A small earring that sits close to the earlobe | A large jewel in a crown | A metal bump on a boot
test: «Dad drove the mare to the stud, where horses are kept for breeding.» What does stud mean here? | A small earring | A bump on a LEGO brick | A small piece of metal on a boot | *A farm where horses are kept for breeding
exam: In «studs-up tackles are fouls», what does stud mean? | *The metal or plastic bumps on the bottom of a boot | A wooden beam inside a wall | A farm where horses are kept | A small earring
